<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vicente Filísola</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1789–1850</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vicente Filísola (1789–1850) was an Italian-born soldier in Spanish and then Mexican service. Sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide">Agustín de Iturbide</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> in 1822, he carried out its annexation to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-mexican-empire">First Mexican Empire</a> and in 1823 took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>, which had resisted. After Iturbide's fall he called the congress that declared Central American independence in July 1823; he later served as Santa Anna's second-in-command in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> in 1836.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vicente-filisola.jpg" alt="Vicente Filísola" width="320" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_Vicente_Fil%C3%ADsola.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
